Reason:
The mutable `FsPermission` class allows changes to its permissions, which can lead to inconsistencies when used as IPC parameters. The ability to modify permissions during IPC calls can cause errors because different processes may operate on different versions of the `FsPermission` object, leading to unpredictable behavior.

The code statements that fix the bug are:
```java
WritableFactories.setFactory(ImmutableFsPermission.class, FACTORY);
return new ImmutableFsPermission(permission);
```
Reason:
The introduction of the `ImmutableFsPermission` class provides a version of `FsPermission` that cannot be modified. By registering this immutable class and returning an instance of it, the patch ensures that IPC operations can safely use `FsPermission` objects without the risk of unintended modifications, maintaining consistency across processes.

